Some of our users need to run fairly cpu-intensive simulations on
our decstation 3100 (running ultrix 4.2).  The runs are taking
extremely long -- 12+ hours.  Recently I ported our simulation
program to a PC (a 486) and ran a test simulation there.  The same
model that took 4 hours on the decstation took 25 minutes on
the PC.  I'd run the 12 hour jobs on the PC too, but the PC runs
out of memory, so it can only handle "short" jobs.  Some of our
users need to run many long jobs (12+ hours) to get good 
statistics.

My problem is that I can't believe such a large discrepancy 
(4 hours vs. 25 minutes) in run times.  I know that ultrix is
ofcourse multi-tasking, and that jobs in the background receive
lower priorities, but I still can't believe the 4hr/25min ratio.
Can anyone suggest some reasons why this may be happening?
Is there anything I can do to improve the performance?

Here are some things I have tried:
1. I've tried (as root) renicing the jobs to a -10 priority
2. I've tried recompiling the program with -O optimization
   (I usually compile it with -g)

Neither attempts improved things -- the 4 hour job still took
4 hours (ps -aux said that the job took about 40 minutes of
cpu time, near the end).  The next thing I was going to try was
to run the job in single user mode for comparison to the PC, but
this would not be an acceptable solution for obvious reasons.

I'd really appreciate some help on this.
Thank you,
Andrew
