In article <CK8G5L.MF@lincoln.gpsemi.com> dsc@pepper.swindon.gpsemi.com (Dave Clarke x8048) writes:
>Hi,
>	I have a question on the back planes H9278 and H9276.
>		What is the main difference between the two ? (apart from the
>number of slots )
>		Does anyone have a list of the signals on the Two back planes ?
>
>	The reason I ask is that I would like to put an old RLV12 into the H9278
>plane so I can transfer some files off of some old RL02's. Or is it easier to put
>an RQDX3 into the H9276.
>

The H9276 backplane is Q-bus on all of the A-B and C-D rows.  The first
three slots of the H9278 backplane have Q-bus on the A-B side and the
so called C-D interconnect on the C-D side.  The C-D interconnect was used
by the RLV-11 controller (M8013/M8014) and the VSV-11 (M???). After those
boards were retired, the C-D interconect became the PMI (private(?) memory
interconnect) used by memory boards in the 11/83 'n stuff.  I think that
all of DEC's quad-wide boards will work in those first three slots, including
the RLV12 (M8061).  Needless to say, dual-wide boards won't work on the
C-D side of the first three slots of the H9278; and you don't need
bus-grant cards in empty C-D slots there.  The remaining slots on the H9278
are Q-bus on both sides and will support both dual and quad boards. 



