In article <2hsfi9$jge@hopi.dtcc.edu> chuck@hopi.dtcc.edu (Chuck Whealton) writes:
> I was told by Emulex that I can only use their disk subsystems (which
> they no longer sell) or the identical (geometry) disks which came in
> those subsystems (e.g. The 110mb [formatted] Maxtor XT-1140 is one).

The XT-1140 isn't a SCSI drive; it has an ST-506 interface.  To use it with
the UC03 you would need a SCSI/ST-506 bridge controller, such as an Adaptec
ACB-4000, although that's probably not the bridge that the UC03 expects.

In principle SCSI devices are just an array of blocks numbered from zero to
some maximum block number, depending on the capacity of the drive.  You might
be able to use a SCSI disk other than what the UC03 was designed for, although
you might not be able to access the whole drive.  On the other hand, the UC03
might depend on being able to use special commands to the bridge board, in
which case you're hosed.

Rather than getting a Maxtor XT-1140, I would recommend either:

1)  Get an XT-2190, which is equivalent to a DEC RD54, and get an RQDX3
    controller.  Skip the UC03 and bridge controller entirely.  There's no
    point to using SCSI if you just convert it back to ST-506 interface.

2)  Get a better SCSI host adapter.  The newer Emulex adapters can handle any
    reasonable SCSI drives.  I've heard that DEC makes a SCSI adapter now but
    I have no idea if it is any good.

Cheers,
Eric


