If what you have are really MicroVAX I, and not VAXstation I, then what
hooks up to them is any kind of character-cell terminal (eg. Digital VTxxx).
These don't have graphics devices.

If you have a VAXstation I, then you need a VR260 graphics monitor,
an LK201 keyboard and a VS10X mouse.

As for disks, the RD5x series (ST506 interface) are appropriate, if you have
the appropriate controller (RQDX1 or RQDX2.)  What boards other than the
two CPU boards are in the chassis?  (Note that the system bus is QBUS.)

I have both a MicroVAX I Owner's Manual and Technical Description manual;
unfortunately they have page sizes that don't photocopy well.  If you send
me mail with your mailing address, I can send you a copy of some relevant
sections; photocopying the entire manuals is a bit more than I can swallow,
and I'm not willing to part with them (they've come in handy a number of
times.)

I *ALSO* just happen to have physical saveset backups of the diagnostics
for the MicroVAX I.  You'd need to somehow restore these onto RX50
floppies (do you even have an RX50 drive?) to use them.

Among other things I have kicking around are a set of PROMs from an early
microcode revision for the MicroVAX I, but I keep these for sentimental value
only, as they're otherwise totally useless.

(Ah, memories.  My workstation, QUARK, has gone through numerous
rejeuvenations, not unlike Dr. Who.  The first one was a prototype MicroVAX I,
which is why I have all this stuff.  Since then it was a VAXstation I,
VAXstation II, VAXstation II/GPX, VAXstation 3100-40 and now 
a VAXstation 4000-60.)

Are you sure you really want to bother with all this?
