dsc@pepper.swindon.gpsemi.com (Dave Clarke x8048) writes:
>	I've just got a VAXstation II but no keyboard/mouse/monitor.
>	What I would like to know is can I easily add an external terminal to the
>9 pin port in the rear and use that, I do have a BCC08 console cable if that is
>needed. 
>	Also, what do the picture symbols mean against the switch at the back of
>the VAXstation, not the baud rate selector switch the other one.

My memory is for a Vaxstation3200, but I think the symbolics and meaning havn't
changed...
Yes, one can connect a terminal to the 9pin console port. But with the presence
of the GPX boards, the CPU startup ROM will still use the graphic interface to
display messages and read the input from the attached keyboard. Here come the
console switches into the play:
The toggle switch is the "break enable/disable" switch. The position marked 
with a dot inside the circle means "break enabled". In this position, the boot 
firmware will
1) halt in a >>> prompt after system tests, continue booting otherwise
2) halt the system upon a BREAK signal from the console port.
Without a screen/keyboard attached to the GPX boards break must be enabled in
order to use the serial console port (to see the system startup or to change
boot parameters).
On the Vaxstation3200 there is also an additional rotary switch with 3
positions: 
arrow: the normal running position
face symbol: firmware will prompt for the console language 
T in a circle: test position for field service use.

>Are their any other major differences between a UVAX II and the
>VAXstation apart from the two VAXstation graphics boards ?
Without the GPX it's identical to a uVAX II. But be aware, the existance of
the GPX interface is used to decide if it's a Vaxstation or a (server) VAX: 
system-software licensing depends on that. At least for VMS You will need a 
new (more expensive) license to run without GPX.
