In article <1994Jan15.005344.6780@mole-end.matawan.nj.us>, mat@mole-end.matawan.nj.us writes:

|> Let's try this:
|> 
|> Designed to operate as the center of a large computing system, including
|> (potentially) a farm with dozens of disk and tape drives, 

I agree so far.
                                                              and with the
|> editing of character I/O relegated to subsidiary processors so that it
|> deals only with completed input requests, _and most commonly used as
|> designed_.

Not a big issue IMHO.  To be debated in some other thread.


Let's try this definition instead:


A mainframe is a computer designed to operate as the 
*data management* node of a large computing system.  
The computer has the following "general" attributes:


1)  Overall, the system is available *to users* at least 97% of 
all wall clock hours throughout the year, and is available 
*to users* 99.5% of all *scheduled* wall clock hours.  This 
includes all downtime associated with filesystem maintenance
(e.g. time to fsck the disks or whatever it takes after a crash),
and includes access to every user data file.  (e.g. it doesn't
meet the criteria if the system is up, but users can't access
their filesystems.)

2)  The system is designed to fail gracefully if possible, with
extensive hardware and software diagnostics, error detection, and
error recovery.  Warnings of impending problems are provided
to users and staff if possible.  The diagnostics and other 
features support a short Mean Time to Repair.

3)  The system is designed to support virtually failsafe
access to users stored data, with extremely minimal
probability of system-caused loss of user data.

The system also has the following time-dependent attributes.
Everything is scaled to 1 "MIPS", and a few rough data points
on the time line are ~1967 = 1 MIPS, ~1980 = 14 MIPS,
~1993 = 200 MIPS.  [I may need to adjust these somewhat.
But you get the idea.  Let us say, something like this:
MIPS ~= 1*10^^((Year-1967)*.088) ]  Why do I think this
scaling is somewhat valid?  Because at best the number 
of MIPS it takes to manage a given amount of disk space 
hasn't changed much over the years.  (At worst, it takes
more "MIPS" to manage a given amount of space than it
used to.)

4)  The system CPUs can deliver a total of at least 200
"VAX-equivalent (VAX 11-780=1.0) "MIPS" to user jobs in
user state.  (1993)  The system is capable of utilizing 
90%+ of the available CPU cycles in either system or user
state, and of delivering 75%+ of the CPU cycles in user
state, while sustaining its rated average I/O load of
1 MByte/sec/MIPS (see below).  The system is capable of
delivering at least 50% of the CPU cycles to user state
while under a sustained heavy swapping load combined with
a sustained heavy "small I/O" (e.g. 2K-4K I/O's) user 
filesystem load.

5)  The system I/O subsystem is capable of delivering at
least 200 MBytes/sec sustained (@ < 25% system state CPU
utilization), and 800 MBytes/sec peak (100% utilization OK),
of I/O through the attached devices and software (e.g. 
including driver/filesystem overhead).  That is, 1 MB/sec
I/O sustained per MIPS and 4 MB/sec peak.  The I/O subsystem
can support enough devices to sustain the average I/O load 
while doing a typical user filesystem load of mixed small 
(mode 2K, avg 8K) reads and writes (probably about 
50% reads, 50% writes these days).

6)  The system is capable of managing at least 200 GBytes of
random access mass storage, and at least 2 TeraBytes of nearline
storage, (including its directories/inodes/catalogs...)
That is, 1 GByte online/MIPS, and 10 GBytes nearline/MIPS.
By "managing", it is implied that the availability criteria
set in #1 above can be met.  That is, the system must be *online*
with the above storage at least 162 hours in any given week.
Crash recovery time, including any subsequent offline filesystem 
recovery/maintenance, is to be subtracted from the uptime.

I believe that the above criteria are probably sufficient for
many data center applications, although transaction processing
is expected to have much more stringent downtime requirements
these days.  (Correct me if I am wrong, but I think people
talk about 99.5%-99.95% total availability, with virtually no
scheduled downtime.

In addition, some data centers may want to support even larger
offline storage access, with offline storage in the 10+ TByte
range.

Item #5 above is *somewhat*, but not completely, arbitrary.  
I have seen studies on various S/W, H/W systems (IBM, DEC, Sun, others, 
TSS, VM, MVS, Unix, others) as well as the interesting information 
posted by Lynn Wheeler in this newsgroup recently.  The range of I/O
required and/or demonstrated, per "MIPS" is quite large.  Also,
the usage pattern for disk storage varies widely, from <10 MB/user
with thousands of users, to 1+ GBytes/user, with only scores of users.  
In general, the more active users and less storage/user, the more 
online devices/arms you need, and the harder it is to get enough arms
without buying too much space.  etc. etc.  In a heavy user I/O
environment, about 1 arm for every 1-10 MIPS is the range.
Assuming about 1 per 3 MIPS, you arrive through extrapolation at
200 MIPS = 67 disks.  In extreme cases, you might have to support 
250+ devices to get enough arms.  (It isn't obvious that disks larger
than 1 GByte are useful, for example, for cases of lots of "little" 
users (less than 10 MB each) doing small (e.g. ~2K) I/O's.  This is a 
good area for more applied/operational research, as well as for tools
which allow systems folks to measure, understand, tune, and manage
the disk space pool.  But I digress.)


There are many other criteria one would like to add, were there time:  
e.g. the ability to support tens of thousands of active accounts,
(some large universities have 30-50 thousand total students and staff),
hundreds, even thousands, of logged in users, security, security *and* 
user access to and management of tapes and other mountable media, 
user response times, etc. etc. 


I could be wrong about this, but I don't think there is a current
RISC-based Unix server with all of the above attributes, although 
some of the larger ones certainly have enough CPU speed, and a few
even have enough memory bandwidth and I/O bandwidth.


