... another measure would be E/B ... or MIPS/bandwidth ratio;
while IO subsystem pathlengths have tended to greatly increase over
the last 20-30 years ... the number of bytes per avg. operation has
also increased. In addition, various types of disk control structures
have migrated from I/O operation to "in-memory" structures.

Transactions (with say a RAID5+) typically represents a significantly
larger number of operations than bulk-striped RAID3. Some
configurations could run into operations/sec limitations
(i.e. pathlength per operation) while others would see bus limitations
(bytes/second).


There tends to be a flavour of "industrial strength" to mainframes
that have:t

	* long MTBF
	* (almost) never loose data
	* lots of failure isolation features
	* lots of failsafe features
	* disaster recovery plans (possibly
	  more a cultural thing than a 
	  software/hardware thing)
	* more attention to scalability and/or
	  graceful degradation
	* trend towards transaction-oriented, atomic
	  operations (disk data structures are never
          allowed to be inconsistent)

there is also growing attention to availability with no single
(in some cases no double, or no triple) points of failure along
with geographical disaster survivability

Sample comparison:

	* no memory parity
	* 1bit/byte memory parity
	* 2bits/byte ECC 
	* 16bits/doubleword ECC
	* 15/16ths Reed-Solomon encoding
