|>	[...] "
|>
|>My questions are:
|>- what is the exact location of this table in LBN 0 ?
|>- what and where are the fields in this table ?
|>- are there any other restrictions in the boot-block (magic bytes at certain
|>  locations, special instructions, etc.) ?
|>- where is the entry-point of the secondary boot image ? (first byte ?)
|>- in which books/documentation should i look to get more information ?

    It is not clear from the above if you have a problem bootstrapping 
    a MicroVAX II system under a supported operating system and from
    a supported device, or if you are just directly interested in the
    details of the bootstrapping sequence for some other purpose(s).
    
    The boot block documentation and the necessary memory layout should
    be included in the KA630 CPU Module User's guide: EK-KA630-UG.  (I
    do not have a copy of this document handy to check on this, though.)

    Under typical circumstances, the MicroVAX II copies the primary
    bootstrap program (VMB) from console ROM into main memory.  VMB
    then goes looking for the secondary bootstrap (SYSBOOT) image on
    tapes, and on removable and fixed disks.  If the secondary bootstrap
    image is not found, the console goes looking for bootblocks on disks,
    tapes and at predesignated locations in (usually PROM) memory, and
    it eventually gets `deperate' enough to go begging on the LAN for a
    downline-load bootstrap via an NI controller.

    If you are attempting to create a boot block on an ODS-II file
    system under the OpenVMS operating system, you can use the OpenVMS
    WRITEBOOT utility.  However, as the MicroVAX II already has a copy
    of the VMB primary bootstrap in the console ROM, it does not normally
    need to utilize the bootblock.

    Further information on the MicroVAX II bootstraping sequence, on VMB,
    and on SYSBOOT, is included in the VMS Internals and Data Structures
    manual.

    Steve Hoffman
