oliver hartmann (UA5W@ibm3090.rz.uni-karlsruhe.de) wrote:

: HELLO.
: I OWN A DS5000/133 AND I HAVE SOME QUESTIONS ABOUT PERFORMANCE.
: THE TURBOCHANNEL OF THE 5000/1XX SYSTEMS IS SAID TO RUN WITH 12.5
: MHZ SPEED, A LITTLE BIT SLOW, I THINK, SOME OTHER BUS SYSTEMS, MUCH
: CHEAPER THAN THE TURBO, RUN  AT 14.5 OR 25 MHZ. 

  The TURBOchannel on the DS5000/xx or DS5000/1xx runs at 12.5MHz and
provides a 50MB/s architectural throughput. It has been benchmarked in
the ~44MB/s range (it's been a long time and I don't remember the exact
numbers). You get a much higher utilization of the architecture than some
other interconnects.


: NOW I ASK FOR MYSELF
: WETHER I'M RIGHT TO SAY THAT THE BUS IS SLOW FOR SOME GRAPHICAL
: APPLICATIONS (KHOROS) OR WETHER I'M WRONG. I USE THE HX GRAPHICS BOARD
: AND THIS USES NORMALLY ONLY ONE SLOT. 

  Depends upon the type of graphics application you want to work with.
It also depends upon what else you're doing with the DS5000/133.

: I USE THE HX GRAPHICS BOARD
: AND THIS USES NORMALLY ONLY ONE SLOT. SOME DAYS AGO I WAS TOLD THAT
: THE PXG+ GRAPHICS CARD USES TWO SLOTS AND PROVIDES A DATA RATE OF 100
: MB/S RATHER THAN THE HX WITH ITS 50 MB/S BURST RATE.
: IF ANYBODY KNOWS MORE ABOUT TURBOCHANNEL AND HAS TIME TO REPLY I WOULD
: BE VERY HAPPY.

  As I said, it's been a while. But, I don't believe that the HX is capable
of doing 50MB/s (I don't recall that the HX does DMA, and PIO (Programmed I/O)
on the DECstations was limited to ~10MB/s or so). And I don't believe the
100MB/s numbr on the PXG+ either. The fastest DEC 3D graphics card available
for TURBOchannel today is the PixelVision (I don't remember the DEC part no.
designation) but you'd need to check with your DEC rep about support on
the DECstations. Or, you could try the Kubota card.

  You'll find a wealth of TURBOchannel information available on DEC's
gatekeeper	16.1.0.2	gatekeeper:/pub/DEC/TriAdd
including the specifications and some presentations on TURBOchannel and
it's performance. However, one thing to keep in mind about the DS5000/xx
and DS5000/1xx series machines is that the CPU daughter card is pretty
much a TURBOchannel option (this is different than the DS5000/2xx series
where that is not the case). The CPU daughter card interface isn't pure
TURBOchannel as the treatment of some of the control signals is different.
But, effectively, the CPU is using the same address/data path to memory
that any of the TURBOchannel options are.

: ANOTHER QUESTION REFERES TO THE NEW R4000 CPU DAUGHTER CARDS. THE
: DS 5000/133'S MEMORY SYSTEM IS A 32 BIT WIDE SYSTEM (IF I'M RIGHT).
: HAS ANYBODY SOME EXPERIENCES ABOUT PERFORMANCE UPGRADING WITH THIS
: NEW OPTION AND: IS IT WORTH TO OBTAIN?

  The CPU to memory datapath is TURBOchannel, which is a 32bit datapath.
I'm not sure what the performance numbers are for the upgrade, but one
thing you may want to check on (depending upon what TURBOchannel cards
you use and whether you have any 3rd party TURBOchannel cards in your
machine) is that the R4000 is a write-back cache and not a write-through
like the R3000 series PUs. I believe that this meant a change in the
device drivers for TURBOchannel options that use DMA. I would imagine
that DEC took care of providing drivers for all of their cards, but you
might want to check for support for any of your 3rd party cards (it
may impact your choice about upgrading).

I hope that helps.

