PC Roadshow Seminar Abstracts
=============================

  L101  PATHWORKS Advanced Configurations for OpenVMS
        Instructor:  Ronnie Cheng
  	
  This seminar focuses on advanced topics for Digital's PATHWORKS for
  OpenVMS (DOS) server product.  The topics covered include server
  architecture, server configuration tuning for better performance,
  understanding PATHWORKS security model, cluster considerations, and
  advanced system administration issues.  In addition, this seminar
  covers migration issues from V4.x to V5.0.  This seminar benefits
  individuals who manages a PATHWORKS for OpenVMS (DOS) environment.

  Topics covered include:
	~ Understanding the architecture
	~ Configuration Tuning
	~ PATHWORKS Security
	~ Comparing V4.x to V5.0 of PATHWORKS
	~ Preparing for the Migration
	~ Features for customization

  Credentials:
  Ronnie Cheng joined Digital Equipment Corporation in 1987 and has
  worked on server software in the Personal Computing Systems Group.  
  As a senior software engineer, Ms. Cheng is responsible for the 
  design and development of the PATHWORKS for OpenVMS server product.

  Prerequisites of Attendees:
  Attendees should have experience with OpenVMS VAX DCL, OpenVMS
  management and DECnet.

  L102  Introduction to PATHWORKS
	Instructor:  Gary Mauler

  This seminar is a basic introduction to the terms, concepts and
  architecture of Digital's PATHWORKS product.  The seminar provides a
  good foundation for individuals who are about to install a PATHWORKS
  network for the first time.  For those who recently installed
  PATHWORKS, it provides an opportunity for clarifying some of your
  unanswered questions about the system and how it works. To get the
  most benefit from a PATHWORKS based network, it is essential to have a
  broad understanding of its features and capabilities.  This seminar
  gets the new PATHWORKS user started along the right path and clears up
  some of the potentially confusing concepts associated with PATHWORKS.

  Topics covered include:
	~ Introduction to PATHWORKS Components
	~ DECnet for MS-DOS and OpenVMS VAX
	~ Installing PATHWORKS for OpenVMS VAX and PATHWORKS for MS-DOS
	~ PATHWORKS Utilities and Tools
	~ Remote File Access
	~ Network Print Services
	~ Security Considerations
	~ PATHWORKS System Administration
	~ Ethernet Adapters
	~ Office Ethernet Cabling

  Credentials:
  Gary Mauler is a senior engineer with Westinghouse Electric
  Corporation. Mr. Mauler has been involved with PC networking since
  1982.  His experience includes Ethernet local area networks as well 
  as wide area network configurations.  He is currently involved in 
  the systems design and administration of a large corporate network
  consisting of various manufacturers' PCs connected to many types of
  OpenVMS VAX server configurations.

  Prerequisites of Attendees:
  A general background of PCs and VAXes would be helpful but is not
  strictly required.

  L201  PATHWORKS Advanced Configurations for MS-DOS
	Instructor:  Ronald Kuhn

  This seminar focuses on advanced topics for Digital's PATHWORKS for
  DOS product for networking PCs.  The topics covered include advanced
  PC hardware configurations, PC driver installation, PC memory
  management, custom start-up files, In addition, this seminar covers
  migration issues from V4.x to V5.0.  This seminar benefits individuals
  who currently manage and use PATHWORKS for MS-DOS.

  Topics covered include:
	~ PC Hardware Configuration
	~ PC Driver Installation
	~ Memory Management
	  ~~ Extended memory
	  ~~ Expanded memory
	~ Custom Startup Files
	~ Comparing V4.x to V5.0 of PATHWORKS
	~ Preparing for the Migration
	~ Features for Customization

  Credentials:
  Ronald Kuhn is the network manager with the Control Division of 
  Upjohn Company. Mr. Kuhn has been involved with DECnet networking 
  since 1979.  His experience includes Ethernet local area networks 
  as well as wide area network configurations.  He is currently 
  involved in the systems design and administration of a large network 
  of PCs connected to various servers using Digital's PATHWORKS 
  (PCSA) solution.

  Prerequisites of Attendees:
  Attendees should have experience with MS-DOS V3.2 or later, and
  DECnet.

  L202  PATHWORKing Microsoft Windows
	Instructor:  Brad Cooper

  This seminar is directed at system managers, administrators, and users
  who want to understand and make the most of Microsoft Windows.
  Emphasis on the use of PATHWORKS to integrate and manage personal
  computers running Microsoft's windowing interface.  An overview of
  MS-DOS as it relates to the MS-Windows operating environment is
  covered.

  The seminar then focuses on the different methods of managing Windows
  in a network environment.

  Topics covered include:
	~ Why MS-Windows
	~ What It Takes
	  ~~ Hardware
	  ~~ Software
	~ Configuring PATHWORKS for DOS Clients
	~ Windows Configuration Options: Local vs Remote
	~ Installation
	~ Windows' Modes
	~ Tuning Windows
	~ User/Workstation Independence
	~ Dealing with INI Files
	~ Diskless Workstations
	~ DOS Applications Under Windows

  Credentials:
  Brad Cooper is a consultant with ISA's Chicago based Advisory Services
  Group. Over five years experience with a 1,000+ user network and other
  PATHWORKS related projects have taken Mr. Cooper throughout the world.
  His computer interests began with a Digital PDP. He has been involved
  in microcomputers since the CP/M days, computer instruction and
  support since version 1.0 of PC-DOS, graphical operating systems since
  1983, and Digital PC integration products since the VAXmate and PCSA's
  initial release.

  Prerequisites of Attendees:
  Experience administrating a PATHWORKS network and using both MS-DOS
  and Windows is assumed.

  L301  Advanced Memory Topics for MS-DOS and PATHWORKS
	Instructor:  Chris Lord

  This seminar is designed for system administrators and users who need
  to understand memory issues on personal computers, particularly in
  networked environments using Digital's PATHWORKS for DOS software.

  It includes a guided tour through the evolution of the personal
  computer from early 8088 systems to today's 80486 machines.  The
  various specifications and memory architectures are examined in detail
  as well as the problems they solve and the problems they create.
  The seminar includes numerous case studies demonstrating optimization
  techniques including configuration with PATHWORKS for DOS, Windows,
  MS-DOS 5.0 and LAN Manager.

  Topics covered include:
	~ The Architectures: Processors and Memory
	~ The Specifications: EMS, EEMS, XMS, VCPI and DPMI
	~ The Problems: MS-DOS, PC Architecture, Applications, Drivers, 
	  TSRs and Networking
	~ The Solutions: Memory Managers, DOS Extenders, Windows 3.1, 
	  MS-DOS 5.0
	~ The New Problems: Memory Managers, DOS Extenders, Windows 3.1,
	  MS-DOS 5.0
	~ The Magic: Probing, Tweaking, Tuning and Debugging

  Credentials:
  Chris Lord is a principal software engineer with the Personal
  Computing Systems group at Digital Equipment Corporation in Littleton,
  Massachusetts.  Mr. Lord has been involved with PCSA since 1986 and
  has over 10 years of experience with personal computers and MS-DOS.

  Prerequisites of Attendees:
  Attendees should have a working knowledge of MS-DOS and PCs.
  Experience with PATHWORKS for DOS would be very helpful, but is not
  strictly required.

