In my quest for a Intel-based machine to run NeXTSTEP on, I was
looking at the DECpc XL line.  These machines have a PCI bus, and
can be upgraded from a 486 to a Pentium to even an Alpha chip by
replacing a daughter board that has the CPU on it.

HP recently announced it's Gecko machines, which will also have
NeXTSTEP supported on them.  As a person who wants to be able to
develop and test on the multiple architectures that NeXTSTEP will
run on, it'd be useful to have this hardware around too.  Useful,
but given that NeXTSTEP should also be on SPARC hardware by the
end of the year, my bank account obviously can't afford to keep
going out and buying a whole new machine for each architecture
that NeXTSTEP will be available on.

These two events got me thinking.  Would it be possible to build
machines which had multiple CPU's of *different* architectures,
perhaps even running at the same time?

Let's say there was a machine which had both Intel and one of the
RISC-based chips in it.  For a NeXTSTEP developer, one could flip
a switch (or something) to determine which architecture the machine
would boot off of.

In fact, perhaps NeXTSTEP could take advantage of both processors
being there.  So, if I've booted up "in Intel", to test my program
on that architecture, NeXTSTEP could be running the windowserver
on the architecture that I'm not using.  And if I want to run my
programs on the RISC-chip, NeXTSTEP could offload that chip by
running the windowserver on the Intel chip.

Now I realize the market for multi-architecture machines to fulfill
the fantasies of cross-platform NeXTSTEP developers is going to be
pretty small.  However, when talking about a workstation which had
both RISC and Intel processors, there would be another marketing
advantage to the box.  Other native operating systems could ignore
the Intel chip, but it would be useful for running all those legacy
DOS and Windows applications.

Apple intends to have a card for Macs which would have an Intel
chip, just to run DOS/Windows apps on Macintosh hardware.  Have
any RISC workstations thought of providing the same kind of
hybrid machine?

Would this be a great idea, or is everyone out there laughing
hysterically at this point?  I'd like to have access to the
multiple architectures, but I don't want to have to buy two
monitors, two keyboards, and two of everything else to do it.

--
Garance Alistair Drosehn     =     gad@eclipse.its.rpi.edu
ITS Systems Programmer            (handles NeXT-type mail)
Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute;           Troy NY    USA


