In article <1994Jan13.134543.6315@aber.ac.uk>,
ccs@aber.ac.uk (Christopher Samuel) writes:
Christopher> Not quite *fully*, all interrupts, system context
Christopher> switches and scheduling is done on one processor, so (on
Christopher> MIPS at least) when the system gets busy you get a
Christopher> bottleneck.  We've got 2 5830's (6000 cabs with MIPS
Christopher> processor boards) and we've found that out the hard way..

As I remember it, the DECsystem 58x0 series is a particularly
pathological case--it was designed by VAX engineers using VAX
technology, and the backplane simply isn't up to the memory bandwidth
demands of the MIPS processors.  This can be seen fairly easily on
compute-bound memory intensive tasks; the problem is sufficiently bad
that you can spot it in the individual specmarks for a single cpu
5800.  Just compare the individual specs for a DS5810 to those for an
R2000 or R3000 based machine with a closer memory subsystem--a
DECstation 3100 or DECstation 5000 will do fine--and the deviations
due to the slow memory are obvious.  As a result, the 58x0s bottleneck
very easily due to a simple lack of memory bandwidth.  I'd be inclined
to believe this is the problem with your 5830s, unless you have some
evidence that it is caused by scheduling or interrupt servicing
bottlenecks.

