>> Sorry, our latest "mainframe" (the 5995M) has the CPU on a single ~11 inch
>> square card.
>
>So what makes it a mainframe... that is, why do you classify it as one?
>
>(related question: is a Sequent a mini or a supermicro?)

Around here, we consider our Sequents to be minis.

The processors (Intel 80X86s) come in pairs on cards about 1-foot
square, with a gazillion chips for interfacing the bus and memory
management (so Peter, does that make it a single-chip CPU?).

I still consider the defining feature of the mainframe to be the ability
to push big I/O across high-speed channels.  And that's not what we do with 
ours.  Our Sequents' job is to rummage around in an Oracle database, maybe 
do some joins, and push a relatively small amount of data out the Ethernet 
controller to the users' terminals.  We use and manage the Sequents in 
roughly the same manner as the same manner as the VAXes they replaced.  I 
think that's still minicomputer territory.

