In article <1994Jan14.104449@rzhp9a.gsi.de>, kaspar@rzhp9a.gsi.de (Matthias
Kaspar) asks about the performance of the following program:

>#include stdio
>#include math
>
>main()
>{
>double x,y=1;
>int i;
>for (i=0;i<200000000;i++) x=sin(y);
>}
>

It runs very fast calling sin (as written) or calling cos but runs much
slower if sin is replaced by tan, sinh, cosh, tanh, or exp.

The reason is a compiler optimization which is able to delete all calls to
sin or cos because x is a dead variable and sin/cos have no side effects.

However, when tan, sinh, cosh, tanh, or exp are called, this optimization
cannot be done because these math functions could have the side effect
of setting the errno variable.  Therefore, in these cases most of the
execution time is spent in the math library which is a shareable image placed
in system space so that it can be easily shared between different
user images.


