Talk to your sales rep.  If you purchased a tape drive from your Digital
sales rep, with the explicit intent to connect it to your InfoServer, and 
your Digital sales rep failed to determine if you had already purchased the 
necessary license to use the tape drive, that is your rep's fault -- that 
is, in my opinion, THEIR JOB -- to sell you equipment you can use at a 
reasonable price.  You obviously can't use this tape drive you purchased.

Be sure you have solid footing, though -- if you just called 1-800-DIGITAL,
and ordered a tape drive, don't expect your local sales rep to help you at
all -- you went out on your own and purchased equipment.  If, on the other
hand, you told your sales rep "I want a tape drive on my InfoServer, what
should I buy?", you've got a case.

If your DEC sales rep doesn't want to make it right, return the tape drive
to him (or his boss) and explain, in writing, why you are returning it.

As to the licensing fee, I don't like it, but, on the other hand, someone 
that has an InfoServer for nothing more than serving CD-ROMs doesn't want 
to have to subsidize those users who have CD-ROMs, tapes, and magnetic
disks on their InfoServer (there is a siginificant amount of coding 
involved to get tapes to work and CD-ROMs to work -- they require different 
drivers on your client system, for example -- this coding isn't free -- 
someone has to get paid to do it).

