DSNlink (which allows you to search Digital's internal database of 
problems, patches, and workarounds for Digital products, as well as submit 
electronic messages to report problems or ask questions [instead of dialing 
the 1-800 number and playing phone-tag]) has always been free -- you only
need to install the software, supply a 2400bps (or faster) modem, and a 
dedicated telephone line.

I haven't heard any rumor that Digital has, or will, start charging for
DSNlink -- this, in my opinion, would be completely against the purpose of 
DSNlink:  by giving the customer the ability to search Digital's support
database, it keeps you from calling and talking to a person.  This is the 
same concept as ATM (teller machines) -- you (the customer) are happier 
because you can get cash anytime day or ngiht, and you don't have to wait 
in line, and the bank is happy because you didn't come into the lobby and 
take the time of one of their cashiers (but the bank charges you $0.50 for
the privilege of saving them money?  Um, hey, wait a minute!).

>So light a fire under your "computer geeks" and see what happens.

They usually run.  Fast.

