In article <1993Jan9.054113.4861@spcvxb.spc.edu>, terry@spcvxb.spc.edu (Terry Kennedy, Operations Mgr.) writes:
> In article <17869@umd5.umd.edu>, bleau@umdsp.umd.edu writes:
>> 1)  I just found out that the 1st SCSI bus on teh VS3100-76 is internal only
>> and can't be extended beyond the system box.  That limits me in that all my
>> additional scsi devices have to go on the 2nd bus.  Can anyone confirm or deny
>> this statement?  I got it from DEC, btw, but you never know.  Also, is there a
>> non-DEC way of feeding it to the outside world?
> 
>   I believe the M76 uses the same SCSI/SCSI adapter as the M38/48. [...]

Based on your description, my VS3100-76 uses a decidedly different arrangement.

The SCSI baseplate still has three mounting positions.  As you described for
the 38/48, the area on the right can be used for a front-panel-mounted 5.25
device like a TZ30, RX2xS, or RRD40 or 42.  OR, it can be used for a 3.5"
internal, just like the two left positions.  In the case of the RX2xS, a rather
large space is also occupied by the SCSI/STnnn adapter.  (If this is removed,
physically there really is room for two 3.5 devices on the right, not just one,
but the mounting holes aren't there.) 

All internal devices in this /76 are run from the scsi A bus.  The B bus
goes directly outside.  

>   As far as getting the A bus out of the box, I wouldn't do it. The cable is
> somewhat unusual in that it loops back into the connector (which has 100
> pins) and the terminators are on the SCSI adapter itself.

Unlike the situation described on the /30 and /38, the A bus here doesn't
loop back into the connector on the SCSI adapter for termination.  Instead it
ends in a *male* Berg-style 50-pin connector.  Inserted in this is a SCSI 
terminator built on a female Berg connector.  

So, you can take a length of 50-conductor flat cable, crimp a 50-pin female
connector onto one end (to mate with the male connector at the end of the
internal SCSI A bus), and whatever else you need on the rest of it.  If the /76
had an RX23 in its front panel (basically useless in my opinion) you can pull
that and its SCSI slave adapter, thus freeing up a position for an internal
drive, and cover the resulting front panel hole with a plate, into which you
install a female Amphenol SCSI connector.  Use standard external SCSI cabling
after that.  

Don't forget a terminator on the end of the "extended" SCSI chain.  

This setup has been working for me just fine for several months now.  I have
three internal drives and two external on the SCSI A bus; at times I've had the
full load of seven drives on it.  The usual cautions about keeping SCSI chains
as short as possible, using as few connectors as possible, etc., of course
still apply, but this particular SCSI bus seems to be pretty tolerant (the
slowness of async SCSI probably has something to do with that). 

Also, if you load up this box with three max-capacity internal drives, you
may want to worry about the power supply capacity.  

