>$200 million? Per year, presumably? Just development and maintenance for what,
>two or possibly three operating systems? I don't believe it. How come TGV came
>to be and yes, still exists and, I suppose, thrives?

Chris is being misquoted concerning the $200M budget. If you look at Digital's
annual report it spends about 10% of its revenue per year on R&D. Revenue
per year is about $14B. 10% of that is $1,400M. $200M of that is about 15% of
the total. This is obviously wrong. Think about how much Digital must spend
on Alpha Chip development and on other software. Developing DECnet is certainly
not going to be 15% of the total R&D spend. Maybe she meant REVENUE but
here $200M seems to me to be a bit on the low side.

Regarding the subject of this notes string. I'm in Digital's Networks 
Engineering Group (I'm not actually in the DECnet/OSI development group
but I know what goes on there).

I have read no end of absolute twaddle and FUD on upgrading to DECnet/OSI
(aka DECnet Phase V). It is NOT a big deal. For most customers there is
no need to install a name server. Upgrading a VMS system to Phase V involves
answering about 6 simple questions. And NO you don't have to upgrade all
your routers. You do need ONE router on your LAN that can run Phase V to
get cluster alias support. Even this is not a big deal.

From a programming and user point of view DECnet/OSI is COMPATIBLE with
DECnet Phase IV. If it isn't Digital will fix it. It even has an NCP
emulator so that most common NCP commands can be used.

Yes it does have a new management utility (NCL). And YES NCL does take
a bit of learning. But most users shouldn't have to touch NCL just as today
only administrators touch NCP.

Yes the HELP supplied with DECnet VAX Extensions on NCL was a pile of crap
but don't base your views on what DECnet/OSI V5.7 (WAVE3) is like useability
wise on what you thought of WAVE1 (DECnet VAX Extensions). Things are much
better.

Personally I think Digitasl has TOTALLY mismarketed the upgrade to Phase V
from Phase IV. It's been interpreted as "Digial is dumping DECnet in favour
of OSI". No, wrong. The old DECnet capabilities are still there, they have just
been augmented by OSI capabilities.

As pointed out Ultrix and OSF/1 have been running Phase V only for ages. They
work fine in a Phase IV network. With OpenVMS it'll be just the same.

Finally. Even for thise folks who have to put up DNS it needn't be looked at
as a real show stopper. Yes it will require planning, but doesn't everything?

Dave


