In article <2h6kuk$60h@crcnis1.unl.edu> mbao@unlinfo.unl.edu (minqi bao) writes:
>Dear Friends:
>
>When I type in "dump 0unf /dev/nrmt0h /usr2" in the root account on
>DECstation 5000/200 with a DEC TLZ04 Tape Drive, where /usr2 is a 
>directory mounted via NFS by the machine atom.unl.edu, the following 
>message appears on the screen and stop:
>...
>  DUMP: Dumping /rusr2@atom (/usr2) to /dev/nrmt0h
>  DUMP: Cannot open /rusr2@atom
>
>I would appreciate it very much if someone could tell me how to dump
>this /usr2 on the tape.
>
>Thanks you very much!
>
>MinQi Bao
>minqi@physics1.unl.edu

You cannot use dump to back-up a partition in this fashion. The way to do
it is to login to the machine which has /usr2 physically attached, and use
'rdump' to dump /usr2 on a remote tape drive. At least this is the case
under Ultrix 4.2a and earlier. This is because 'dump' uses the 'character'
device corresponding to the partition being dumped (it get the corresponding
character device from /etc/fstab, or by adding a 'r' in front of the name
of the block device).

Consequently, the error message that you got says that it could not open
what it thought was the character device.

BTW, I have never tried to NFS export a character device. I suspect that
it might not work.

Philippe Pouliquen
philippe@zeus.ece.jhu.edu


