>  Try none?  These sorts of things are handled by gateways, the distributed
>name servers and similar things that don't require system by system changes...

The gateways can mask some of the change, but not all.  How are end systems
running sendmail going to handle the change without reconfiguration?
(Merely changing any/all instances of "dec.com" to "digital.com" in
sendmail.cf won't cut it if you don't want to bounce mail to the old
address.  Rewriting all incoming "digital.com" mail to be "dec.com"
at the gateways might work, but that means you'll be forever using dec.com
internally.)

If I were running a large mailing list that had lots of Digital people
on it, I'd be very concerned right now over when (or whether) I'm going
to have to start changing those addresses, or whether I'm going to start
getting mail bounces or unrepliable mail due to somebody or other making
some sort of mistake.  Mistakes happen all the time in any change of this
sort, no matter how well-planned and well-coordinated the change may be.

And yes, Gwyn, I'm well aware of pseudo-domains like "mts.dec.com" and
"enet.dec.com" and how easy those will be to change, since they're gateways
to non-SMTP mail transports.  But that doesn't cover the "site.dec.com"
folks who are using SMTP receivers of one form or other.

Even assuming that the changes are isolated only to gateways and nameservers,
that still involves a good number of folks spending some finite amount of
time addressing the issue.  I'd personally rather that Digital spend most
of its time trying to address customer issues rather than spend it making
cosmetic changes to enhance the company's "image".

-Shawn Mamros
E-mail to: mamros@ftp.com
As always, speaking for myself, not my employers...



