Let's see...Novell has an NT Requestor for Windows NT (Intel, Alpha, and MIPS)
that allows a Windows NT station to act as a NetWare client and attach to
NetWare servers.  This SW is still BETA and can be found on ftp.novell.com
in /pub/netwire/novfiles.  Microsoft has an equivalent that uses NDIS 3.0
drivers instead of Novell ODI drivers, but I haven't played with it.

Of course, this doesn't answer your question.  I don't know of any bits that
run on top of Windows NT or OSF/1 that make it look like a NetWare server, but
it's possible that there are some.

I do know of the current Processor Independent NetWare program that will allow
a native NetWare 4.X release to run on non-Intel based machines.  The current
targeted platforms include HP, Digital, and Sun.  We've demoed a DECpc AXP 150
running PIN last year at PC Expo and more recently at NetWorld in Dallas.  I
don't have any specifics to share on when this would become available, but you
can ask your Digital sales rep or Novell contacts.  Note, PIN will be shipping
from Novell in the familiar Red Box, just like the current Intel based versions
do.
