In article <2gvsl7$g83@rox.craycos.com>, Robert Herndon <rh@craycos.com> wrote:
>
>
>The big problem with many operating systems, e.g., VMS, MVS, TSS, PICK,
>...  is that only one vendor provides them.  Thus, if your applications
>are developed for VMS, you'll be stuck buying DEC boxes until you port
>the application to some other vendor's OS.  IBM, and to a lesser extent,
>DEC, have made many fortunes on this.  JHU lost a bundle to IBM this
>way... JHU lost a smaller bundle to DEC this way (were you around for
>the KL-10?).  And so have MANY others.  UNIX is a sane way to attempt
>to avoid losing one's shirt at a future date.

Isn't about time to stop perpetuating this crap?  Even to get a 
program to be portable for different Unix platforms, you've got to
adhere to the standards.  If you use *any* operating system features,
you've lost your portability.  Same is true for VMS, and I suspect
MVS, etc..  If you adhere to the standards and don't use the DEC or
IBM (or whoever) extensions, your code ought to port easily enough.
We've got some code around here that basically runs on just about
anything.

The choice of which O/S to use should *never* have to depend on
whether the O/S is available on another vendor's hardware... guess
what? none of the O/Ss are available on someone else's box.  Sure
there are similarities, but only on a shallow level.  For example, try
to form any unix-like cluster with multiple vendors' risc/unix
platforms, all executing the same copy of code, sharing the same
executables and binary information, providing both performance and
high-reliability, allowing minimal numbers of installations/upgrades
of software and O/S (since those are big benefits of clustering).  It
might be do-able, but it's pretty difficult.  So if I want to
implement something really powerful that also has high availability,
you'll probably be stuck on one vendor's platform anyway.  O/S doesn't
"necessarily" matter (tho that depends on whether you think NFS-based
clusters are superior to VMS-based clusters).

I dare you to tell me how my choice of platform even matters when I
decide to get, say, a relational database.  If I'm concerned about
application portability, then I should only by *software* from vendors
who support them on multiple architectures and O/Ss (Oracle, Ingres,
etc. for Rdbs).  Hardware is independent of this solution.  My
hardware (and my O/S) decisions should solely be determined by what my
*business situation can support*.  As in the above example, trying to
support many seperate unix-like systems has a high cost if I can't
share resources, o/s, executable code, etc..  When I have 2000+
systems to deal with, this become a big deal.  Even at 100-200 this
becomes an issue.  Just saying the word "unix" doesn't solve this
problem any better than saying something else.

There will always be some cases where single point solutions are only
available on one vendor's platform.  If that is my only choice at
that time, then the costs/benefits to my business have to be considered.

>So, even if various commercial OSes have lots of nice features that 
>UNIX doesn't, it is still a very sane and rational reaction to avoid 
>those operating systems.  In choosing them, one is likely to incur 
>substantial future costs should additional performance be needed, 
>because the vendor has the customer over a barrel.  IBM is not the 
>only vendor ever to have taken advantage of this.

Sane? Rational?  By whose definitions? (other than your own)

Some of that may have been true 2-3 years ago, but it's time to wake
up!!!!  This is a competitive world and all vendors (including IBM and
DEC) *are* competing, and it doesn't require a unix-like o/s to be
competitive.  How, for example, does it cost me more for a AS/400, or
DEC AXP/VMS system than it does for, say, PA-RISC, SPARC, etc..  Those
platforms are changing often enough to cause me severe portability
headaches - and "substantial future costs".

Finally, I think one of the big pitfalls in the proposed ideas is that
the unix-like experiences are all on small, mostly single-user (or at
least a small number of concurrent users) systems.  Costs in the 80s
for these types of environments were definitely in favor of the
unix-like systems.  However they didn't have the reliability,
features, or tools that many large shops, with large applications,
need.  Even today, the unix-like systems don't scale up to large shops
all that well, but newer tools are helping this get better all the
time.

Instead of telling everyone that "unix" is the answer, one should
first find out the question.

jls


