In article <3JAN199411334495@jhuvms.hcf.jhu.edu>, ecf_stbo@jhuvms.hcf.jhu.edu (look out, here he comes again) writes:
> 
>It's well known VMS gives you similar scaleability, but this fact is easily and
>quickly overlooked by the 'market forces' of the zealot bandwagoneers of UNIX
>hegemony intent on forcing 'unix must and will be used for everything by
>everybody; unix of course, now what was the question?' up our asses. Computing
>these days is 95% politics and marketing and 5% technical. But perhaps it
>always has been. 
> 
 IMHO, there is nothing wrong with VMS per se, it has some outstanding capabil-
 ities, etc, but, as you say, "market forces" have pushed many people to UNIX
 in spite of those factors.  But those "market forces" aren't quite what you
 have made them out to be; they have been more as follows:

  1. VMS was available only on DEC VAX processors;

  2. DEC didn't attempt to make VMS available to other vendors who would have
     had a hell of a time "porting" it to other processors in any case;

  3. DEC VAX systems fell behind the performance curve.   But DEC continued to
     charge an arm and a leg for them anyway.  It also played various marketing
     games vis a vis its customers to maximize its profits above and beyond the
     call of duty.  For example, DEC refused to give uVAX II owners an upgrade
     path to the uVAX III for the first 18 months or so that the latter was
     out: the only "choice" was to purchase a complete system, with Yet Another
     TK drive, RD/RF disk, BAnnn cabinet, and OS license;
     
  4. meanwhile, new upstart hardware vendors went with UNIX because a) it was
     available and b), for an operating system, it was quite portable.  Beat
     trying to write a new one from scratch in any case;

  5. one of these upstarts - SUN - began to make serious inroads into various
     customer sites.  Others, such as MIPS, didn't do quite as well but collec-
     tively, they increasingly made VMS and other single-vendor options look 
     expensive and slow.

  Ok, things were quite more complicated than that.  A 100+ million Intel-based
  micros entered the picture, for example.  But this is the heart of the
  "market forces" that were at work, not some UNIX-centric conspiracy.

  We didn't need "zealots of UNIX ... to force [it] up our asses"; it was 
  little things like $65K for a uVAX III vs. $35K for a MIPS box with 3-4 times
  the throughput on our applications that did the trick.  If DEC VAX/VMS was
  price-competitive, or even somewhat close to it during the mid- to late-
  1980's, a lot more people might have gone with it/stayed with it.  But it
  wasn't.

  At this point, DEC Alpha/VMS is very much competitive (a lot of people assume
  that it is because of all the UNIX activity ....).  But it still only runs
  on one vendor's systems - as far as I know - and it is too late in the game 
  for an OS to be in this position and succeed, in my opinion.  By "succeed", I
  mean grow its installed base rather than fading away with the one it has 
  managed to accrue up to now..... 


  greg pavlov
  pavlov@fstrf.org


