In article <2gb1rf$35k@cmcl2.NYU.EDU> liuyu@acf2.nyu.edu (liuyu) writes:

>What's the typical HD size and RAM come with one of these?  Are HD's SCSI?

Most systems use the RD series drives, from 20 MB to 140 MB.  These are not
SCSI.  The system holds either 1 or 3 hard disks (depending on what size box
it is in) plus a floppy or TK 50; it used to be possible to get external RD
series disks too.

You could also hook up RA series disks with the right controller, but you
don't want to.

There are Q bus SCSI controllers.  They used to be expensive.  MIT used
to use them to run Exabyte tape drives on VAX 3600s (we later switched to
VAXstation 3100s with built in SCSI as backup servers).

The CPU is limited to 16 MB memory.  The uVAX II has 1 MB RAM on the CPU
card and can have 2 memory cards added (1, 2, 4, 8 MB sizes).


