cpc@shell.portal.com (Chris Cebelenski) writes:
> [uVAX II == uVAX 2000]
>
>  Strickly from the CPU stand-point, yes, it is.  The system lacks some
> of the buss expansion of the true MVII, but for most purposes it looks and
> feels just like a MVII to the average user.  I don't like the packaging
> as much, but it takes up less space.  Drive options were/are more limited
> also.

By that argument, a 6210 and a 3100 are the same thing.

The II is different from the 2000 in a lot of ways, particularly in disk I/O
and video (if in the VAXstation models), and that affects performance.
I have two RQDX3s in my II, and it helps the system a lot.  (MFM disks tend
to require the undivided attention of their controller -- a quick test shows
that copying between controllers is nearly twice as fast as copying between
two disks on the same controller.)  Also, the RQDX3 has its own micro-
processor whereas the uVAX 2000's CPU has to do more work.  (I haven't
benched the two to see how much differennce this makes.)  On the flip side,
a mono VS2000 is faster than a mono QVSS based VSII, because the 2000 has
a 32 bit path to the video frame buffer whereas the QVSS is addressed over
the much slower (than the CPU) 16 bit Qbus.  Colour graphics adapters
change the picture a bit.  I could go on.

Really, a system is a lot more than just a CPU & clockspeed.  A 2000 is a
really different animal from the II.  

