I maintain and freely provide a program called lsof -- for LiSt
Open Files.  It is a descendant of a freely distributed program
(found in the comp.sources.unix archive), called ofiles, and the
fstat program from 4.2BSD-Tahoe.  Lsof displays files open by active
processes.  It displays information about network files (sockets),
NFS files, and regular files, and its filters enable searching for
specific instances of open files.

Currently lsof runs on a number of Unix OS versions, including AIX
for the RISC/System 6000, HP-UX for the HP 700 series, NeXTStep
for the NeXT work station, and SunOS and Solaris for Sun work
stations.  (NeXTStep, by the way, is BSD Unix layered on Mach, so
I know that a port to that kind of Unix system is possible.)  Now I
am trying to port it to OSF/1 for the DEC Alpha series.  My
development system is running version 1.3 -- uname says "V1.3 112".

At the advice of someone already familiar with DEC OSF, I am trying
to use the table(2) function to access process and user structure
information.  The user structure should lead to a list of the files
open by the process through its u_ofile[] table of file structure
pointers, and the current working and root directory vnode pointers.
Unfortunately, the pseudo user structure that the TBL_UAREA option
of table(2) returns has values in neither the u_ofile[] table, nor
u_cdir, nor u_rdir.

Without these file structure and vnode pointers, lsof cannot be
ported to DEC OSF.  Is there anyone reading this list with sufficient
knowledge of the internals of the DEC OSF kernel who can help me
find a way that lsof can obtain these values?  Should I be trying
to read the actual proc table and its associated utask structures
via /dev/kmem instead?

I would appreciate replies via email to <abe@cc.purdue.edu>.

Thanks in advance.

Vic Abell


