In article <CJ2E47.IIM@biles.com>, daniels@biles.com (Brad Daniels) writes:
> >
> Actually, he's looking at 390 ppm.  It occurs to me, though, that this
> application actually lends itself pretty well to distribution.  If you're
> talking 17ppm HP printers, 390 ppm =~ 23 printers.  You could definitely
> handle it by hardwiring each printer to a minimal workstation, .....

> You could probably get together a complete setup like that for around
> $400-$500K including some custom programming (though depending on the
> complexity of the application, programming could run several hundred thousand
> more), with an annual service bill from $50-$100K. .....

  On a much, much, much smaller scale, we are dumping a "large" printer for
  3 smaller ones, HP 4Sii scale, so in principle this makes sense to me.

  But 23 printers trying to crank 1700 pages per hour each, using toner carts
  good for 8K or so copies, 2000 page input feeders, a choice of a minimal 
  output tray (top of the printer) or a less-than-optimal "large capacity" 
  output capture mechanism, may be more hassle than its worth.  It almost seems
  to be an exercise to prove that a distributed solution can do it rather than
  being the appropriate solution.  I don't really know.  But my druthers would 
  be to purchase another high-capacity printer or two, purchase a set of fast
  system boxes, one for each of the 4-5 printers (you may need five if its true
  that no cheap box can drive a single one of these printers at its rated
  speed), and stop there.  My guess is that this would be the cheaper solution
  in the long run.  HP's are very nice and can be"pushed" well beyond their
  rated duty cycle, but from what I can remember from a vendor/printer survey
  3 years ago, the large printers claim a significantly lower cost per page
  (factor of 2-3 ?) than the HP's.  I think that other costs - labor, for 
  instance - will be lower as well. Basically, this is a high-volume batch-
  style application, so why not  implement it as such ?  If the big printers
  were handling all student/faculty printing for the entire campus, then 
  buying a large batch of HP's and scattering them around campus would seem
   to be the right thing to do.  Tho that's a maintenance headache as well...





