avk@hafnium.cchem.berkeley.edu (Tony Konashenok) writes:


>I wouldn't say uVAX II is much slower than a 386. By absolute performance,
>it is somewhere between a high-end 286 and a low-end 386. On the other hand,
>it is much more robust and rugged than a PC, in both hardware and software
>aspects. Speaking of design quality, VAX architecture is tremendously
>better. So, even though a stripped-down uVAX II can be had for $200, you're
>getting much more bang-per-buck than in an IBM clone.

  Another key is the cost of expandibility. You can set 16 Mbytes of RAM in
the machine for less than a single 4 Meg SIMM for a PC (or not much more).
The VAXstation 1 I got (now a VAXstation 2 running on a borrowed RD53)
came with a 19" 1024x864x1 monitor/framebuffer. Added bonuses include the
"wow" factor of being able to say you have a VAX (to those who know what
one is... ;-), listening to dual on-board fans kick in, having a computer
with an impressive array of buttons on the front panel, and having some
form of compatibility with "big" computers. For students (like me...),
they are indespensible tools for schools that require classes in VAX
architecture. Imagine: "The VAX is down"   "Oh. Want to use mine?"

  Seriously, it's a very nice machine for very little money. The VS1 is
probably the cheapest workstation I've seen (mine was $145 including CPU
and memory upgrade, but no disk), and is a terrific single-user machine.

  Speaking of which, did a MicroVAX-at-home list ever get off the ground?
If so, I have to sign up!

     -- Patrick



