> Megan (mbg@world.std.com) wrote:
> : jfc@athena.mit.edu (John F Carr) writes:
> 
> : >The MicroVAX II started shipping in 1985.  It is a .9 MIPS/SPECmark machine,
> : >slightly slower than a VAX 11/780 (1.000 SPEC) and much slower than a 386 (2-10).
> : >There were 3 packages: two deskside boxes with a Q bus and a desktop system
> : >called the MicroVAX 2000 (which came out in 1987).  DEC sold upgrades to a
> : >faster processor (3-5 MIPS) for the Q bus systems starting around 1989.
> 
> : Even if the MV2000 is based on the same CPU as in the MicroVAX-II (I
> : don't that it is or isn't), the MV2000 is NOT a Microvax-II.
> 
>  Strickly from the CPU stand-point, yes, it is.  The system lacks some
> of the buss expansion of the true MVII, but for most purposes it looks and
> feels just like a MVII to the average user.  I don't like the packaging
> as much, but it takes up less space.  Drive options were/are more limited
> also.
> : 					Megan

The *big* difference between the MVII and the MV (or VS) 2000 is that the built-
in MFM disk "controller" was *not* DMA, but programmed I/O.  Any disk (or tape,
if present) operation required CPU intervention, such that while it was
controlling the disk, it wasn't doing anything else.  I beleive that the same
was true for the ethernet "controller."

