In article <2gmvfj$249@hopi.dtcc.edu>,
Chuck Whealton <chuck@hopi.dtcc.edu> wrote:
>Howdy!  Somebody told me there was a posting that I apparently missed,
>which they *THOUGHT* was actually posted to one of the pdp11 groups,
>on how to build/customize a setup/cable to use a regular VT100 as a
>terminal for a VAXstation 2000.

Create a cable with one 9F and one 25M d-sub, configured like this:

9F	25M
 1  --   1
 2  --   3
 3  --   2
 4  --   5
 5  --   6
 6  --  20
 7  --   7
 8  -\
 9  -/  (8 and 9 connected to each other)

Now you have a standard 25-pin RS232 (DTE) port. Having pin 8 and 9
connected tells the VS2000 that you want it to use this port as console
instead of the real one. The console is always at 9600 baud, btw. Use
no parity, one stop bit.

Note that you have to have pin 8 and 9 connected at powerup, since this
redirection is done by hardware.

Good luck!

/tg



