I suspect that dec.com is larger than uml.edu, and that there are a
lot more sites that have DEC doing some level of DNS for them (I see
an awful lot of people with uucp-gw-1.pa.dec.com as a secondary).
Trying to coordinate a lot of people to do anything can be a task.
Then there's those programs that use hostnames for some sort of
authentication that might be caused grief by a dec.com->digital.com
conversion -- Usenet news comes to mind.  Then you have to take into
account the relative competence involved -- owing to distributed
administration, DEC likely has some people who are more competent and
some people who are less competent.  Then there's the expense in
internal documentation and support.  And of course, it's not always
easy to tweak all mailers "out there", particularly if you're wanting
a system to accept mail destined for "site.blah.digital.com" as well
as "site.blah.dec.com".  Next thing you know, they'll want to rename
the company to OpenSystems Equipment, or something equally obnoxious.

Can anyone from DEC confirm/deny what's going on with this rumored
"renaming"?  Anyone from organizations that have merged or divested 
(causing name changes) care to relate any stories of how easy this is?

