Well, as one of the people who has to deal with our mail gateways (I'm not the corporate expert,
they are at NSL in Palo Alto; CRL acts as backup for them if they go down), I understand the story
is that a study was done of customers and Digital found that many people thought DEC and Digital 
were two different companies.  This isn't good.  Digital has decided to attempt to clarify this,
and Digital was the choice of the two obvious possibilities.

We have literally 10's of thousands of systems that run TCP/IP these days, and even more systems
that are also mail accessable (any of the DECnet systems in the company can be reached 
via the .enet.dec.com sub-domain), and at least 4 mail transports in common use (SMTP, DECnet, 
whatever All-In-One does, X.400).  A bigger hassle than just the name change in the name 
server is getting the mail configurations and header rewriting done correctly, so that all mail
will then come from .digital.com.  To give you an idea of the scale of our mail systems,
there are something like 5 or more regional mail concentrators in various parts of the
world, where incoming or outgoing mail to or from the internet is relayed.  And while
converting, we have still deal with .dec.com mail correctly.  Our gateway mail load is over 700K 
messages/month, and it is essential for our business.

So I believe the conversion will happen, but it will be gradual over quite a while.  Or at least
that is what I've been told.
					- Jim


