In article <3JAN199411334495@jhuvms.hcf.jhu.edu>, ecf_stbo@jhuvms.hcf.jhu.edu (look out, here he comes again) writes:
|> In article <1994Jan01.154825.9568@ksmith.com>, keith@ksmith.com (Keith Smith) writes...
|> >>	Sorry, I have to:  Which provides your business with the best market
|> >>		advantage; being fast or being UNIX???  :-)
|> >Being UNIX of course, because it makes my business computing investment
|> >SCALEABLE from 386/25 up to ALPHA.  
|> 
|> It's well known VMS gives you similar scaleability, but this fact is easily and
|> quickly overlooked by the 'market forces' of the zealot bandwagoneers of UNIX
|> hegemony intent on forcing 'unix must and will be used for everything by
|> everybody; unix of course, now what was the question?' up our asses. Computing
|> these days is 95% politics and marketing and 5% technical. But perhaps it

[Aside first:]
Both VMS, MVS, etc. have capabilities which Unix does not have.  The
market forces which drove Unix into the #1 technical computing
position were its portability and its cost, not its capabilities.  
Remember that 15-25 years ago, IBM and sometimes its competitors, 
the BUNCH, were able to command 10X+ markup on manufacturing cost 
to sales price.  As late as the mid-80's, 5X was the minimum acceptable.  
(I don't know what markups are now, but given the prices I have seen
lately, they must be much smaller.  2X?)  That revenue stream supported 
a lot of R&D, a lot of systems programming, etc.  It also supported 
extremely bloated marketing organizations that sent teams of 5-10 
marketeers out to talk to customers.  

Unix's portability allowed users to pick among several competitors.
Customers were not locked in forever.  Price competition!  Unix also
was cheaper itself in the early days than the extremely expensive
vendor proprietary operating systems.  Until even a couple of years
ago, many/most vendors expected to be able to charge extremely high
fees for vendor-proprietary systems.  It isn't surprising, therefore,
that many customers chose the cheaper, least-common-denominator O/S,
Unix.  Any vendor which expects customers to buy its private O/S is
going to have to show price/performance advantages over Unix.  It is
that simple.
[End aside.]


The more interesting discussion (to me) is about the differences between
Unix and the alternatives in the O/S market.  Clearly robust, high-volume
I/O is one of them.  Historically, Unix was faster, while vendors O/S's
tended to offer higher total multiprogramming throughput.  Unix faster?!?!
That's right.  On most benchmarks I saw during the mid-80's when we were
making such comparisons, Unix was usually faster on single-job synchronous
I/O than vendor O/S's.  The vendor O/S's were usually better at "throughput" 
workloads with lots of simultaneous processes each doing a little bit of
I/O, or single jobs doing multiple streams of asynch. I/O.  Vendor O/S's
were extremely heavily optimized to do the minimum number of instructions
per I/O.

Unix was also faster on benchmarks which happened to require lots of
"little" CPU-bound  programs to be run, because the Unix method of running 
little programs, fork-exec, turned out to be more efficient than loading
programs into an existing address space and running them there.  Or,
at least, Unix was optimized to do this, because it was faster in practice.

What advantages did the vendor O/S's have?

-  Better scheduling, better system resource management, better
   multiprogramming (MP) support.  Better multiprocessing support.
   Sometimes, Symmetric Multiprocessing (SMP). My gut feel is that 
   the better resource management/scheduling is good for about a 2X 
   price/performance advantage for a given interactive workload/
   response time.  (Remember, the cost advantage for Unix was often 
   much more than this.  You could afford to throw away 50% of 
   CPU utilization and still be cheaper.  In effect, vendor pricing
   of proprietary systems acted to ration CPU time, and therefore
   it had to be used with extreme efficiency.)

-  Better multiprogramming performance in the face of not enough
   memory, CPU, or I/O capacity to deal with instantaneous demand.
   (Even today, on some (most?  all?) workstation-class Unix systems, 
   a single large-memory I/O-heavy job will take over all system 
   resources and leave other processes just trickling through.)
   Ability to handle voluminous asynch. I/O from multiple
   competing jobs.

-  More robustness in the face of operational issues, lots of
   users, etc.  Vendor O/S's had to work with thousands of
   potential users and hundreds logged on at any time.  Most
   Unix systems were not designed for this.  They thought
   10 users was about right, and 30-40 a "really big" number.
   20,000 users with 300-400 active?  Forget it.  Yet, you
   could do this with vendor O/S's on some systems with maybe
   a measly 7-14 "SPECint equivalent MIPS". Better operational 
   tools, better operator support, better error tracking and 
   recovery.

  (Some people might count most of the above 1-3 as "scalability".)

-  Better device support.  Most Unix implementations didn't
   support tapes very well at all, for example.  Many still
   don't.

-  More robust filesystems which could recover quickly from
   crashes.


What were the advantages of Unix?

-  Efficient running of "little" programs.

-  Faster single-user I/O.

-  Pipes, and pipe-oriented commands and user interface (shell).

-  Portability.  Competition among vendors.

-  Lower cost, even after taking into account its poorer MP
   performance.

-  4.2 BSD with built-in TCP/IP support.  (TCP/IP turned out to be a
   surprisingly potent advantage for BSD Unix systems.)


Given the seeming advantages of the vendor-proprietary O/S's, it
might be surprising how successful Unix was.  I guess the relative
advantages of Unix turned out to be greater than one might have guessed.  
Especially since some of them tend to work together.  e.g.  With
good networking on BSD systems, you could afford to buy more
small computers instead of one big one and take advantage of the
lower cost.  By having several computers, you could more easily
isolate nasty jobs like databases, overcoming some of the O/S
MP limitations,  Single users were sometimes pretty happy
with their single-user performance on small systems.  With multiple
computers already, it was easy to phase in new systems from
other vendors.  etc. etc.

I am still in the camp which says that many capabilities of big-iron
vendor-O/S's are still needed (e.g. support for large numbers of users, 
SMP, better MP performance, better scheduling, better memory
management, voluminous I/O, asynch I/O, robustness, etc.  the whole 
list really).  But, I think that anyone who thinks that *most*
people will pay 10X to get those advantages is obviously very, very 
wrong.  Granted, there are still *Billions* of dollars to be made on 
existing proprietary O/S's, and some customers need them, but most 
people will get by without the advantages to save money.  The listed 
advantages will have to be incorporated in the low-cost product lines 
of today.  

My $0.02.

