Yes. VAX PSI V4.3 for example.

> I believe the board was being used to speak X.25 in the uVAX II it came
> out of.  Unfortunately, the contents of that system are long gone (one
> of the conditions of taking it away was that the disks got scrubbed, and
> they did), so I can't really check what drivers it was using.
> Anyone know more about this beast?

The WAN device driver kit used to know it as a KMV1A (in its QBUS version).
It was replaced by the DSV11 some years ago, which is why the KMV1A is no
longer in the WAN kit. The KMV1A had (I think) 1 * 19.2K channel and did
level 2 on board (the DSV11 has 2*64K or 1*256K) and does not do level 2.

I used to have many KMV1As. They were unreliable c**p. If the line bounced
more than a couple of times, they would hang until their microcode was
reloaded (done by dumping their contents to NL: with NCP). The KMV was
(I seem to remember) a CSS product designed in Annecy (France), where that
other QBUS nasty, the DHV11, came from.

Having griped about all that, they do work quite well in low stress
environments. Where you will get a kit from though, Heaven knows...

