In article <CJ82IC.nz7@serv4n57.aix.kingston.ibm.com> bksmith@clnt1n60.aix.kingston.ibm.com () writes:
>In article <CJ7IsH.5E2@rahul.net> dhesi@rahul.net (Rahul Dhesi) writes:
>>Has anybody tried defining 'mainframe' recently?  It used to be that
>>the classification of mainframe versus mini versus micro was based
>>solely on price.
>>
>>Now it appears that 'mainframe' has come to mean 'a machine with very
>>high I/O cpaacity'.  If so, this entire discussion is moot, isn't it?
>>By definition, a mainframe will do better I/O than a non-mainframe.
>>
>>What's a good definition of 'mainframe'?
>
>The current crop of machines/manufacturers, that the current PC's and
>desktops are trying to replace.  8-)

Heh. As good a definition as any (but I'll attempt another anyway).

I think this discussion really has shed some light on the word 'mainframe.'

Remember it all started (or quickly became, anyway) a discussion of
I/O bandwidth. 

Well, the way things are going, or being driven, we're going to see ripping
high I/O bandwidths on 'non-mainframes' pretty soon.

Fact is, 'mainframes' are kind of like art ("I don't know what art is,
but I know it when I see it") for many of us.

So I'd like to suggest that 'mainframe' refers to a machine which combines
two things in one box:

	1. High bandwidth for *directly attached* secondary storage
	   devices.
	2. High *general purpose* data-processing capabilities.

Okay, I have to justify this before people start taking shots :-)

(1) is there because I wanted to eliminate things like big task-oriented
boxes on a distributed or client/server network. I want the 'classical'
big-box-hooked-to-a-dasd-farm. Not a machine with twenty FDDI connections
but limited local I/O.

(2) is there because to me, something like a giant NFS file-server
isn't really a mainframe. It's a player in the whole distributed
data environment. It really isn't capable of, if you will, "a
complete thought." The reason I say 'capability' is that an Amdahl
5995M which is hooked to a big DASD farm and is being sucked up 
by NFS file-serving is, I guess, still a mainframe. Even though
it isn't really being used as such. So perhaps some of it is 
'designers intent,' too.

So maybe there should be a third, but softer factor:

   3. Intended for use in supporting multiple and/or diverse
      applications on a single, large, data-processing and
      storage platform.

I know we can toss all kinds of 'buts' and 'what ifs' in there as
red-herrings. But I think that if the above isn't a definition it
at least explains what we're thinking about when we say the m-f
word.

Something critical here, too, is the level of abstraction at which
I/O tends to be done. Seems to me that the evolution of I/O has
really evolved beyond the open-read-write-close basics. We're
tending to need to couple a locality factor that goes beyond
pointing to 'which data-set on this little island' and evolves
towards 'where in this universe.' The nifty thing is that, once
we know the answer to the second part, it still looks like it's
on our little island (until we realize that it's being poled 
across  on a raft rather than being sent in a speed-boat).

However, where all this stuff is leading us, in the applications
world (once the vendors get it working) is away from looking
at 'a record' and towards looking at 'object X which is a representation
of abstraction A,' and actually being able to make the latter model
stick, even if the concrete representation of the abstraction is
one which requires assembling bits and pieces from all over the
world. And, if the application screws up, it at least won't voilate
the integrity of the abstraction. To me, that is the reason to get
beyond 'mainframes.' 

The mainframe model is 'everything is done here.' It isn't adequate
for most things.

Greg


