In article <CJ7IsH.5E2@rahul.net>, dhesi@rahul.net (Rahul Dhesi) writes:
|> Has anybody tried defining 'mainframe' recently?  It used to be that
|> the classification of mainframe versus mini versus micro was based
|> solely on price.

I used to joke that "supercomputer" meant a machine that cost
$3M-$20M.  That seemed to really bug people who have a mystical
belief attached to the word "supercomputer".  Especially since I
was a supercomputer advocate.  Today, many people have a similar
irrational hatred towards "mainframes."

Mainframes certainly fit into a logically-defined price/performance
niche historically.  Eventually, the logic disappeared, but the
marketing tradition lived on.  A lot of salesmen would rather lose
their jobs than lower their margins, it seems.

As I have seen the term used by self-described mainframe bigots over
the last 15 years, "mainframe" has always meant "Big I/O".  It also
was assumed that, going along with the "Big I/O", were a number of
RAM/RAS/... features making for a system suitable to be shared by
hundreds of users...  In other words, a datacenter machine, managed
by one group for the benefit of its computer-naive customers.

For example, when the mainframes were supposedly under attack by
the VAX, ca. 1980, various papers were circulated showing that
small mainframes which had comparable single-job speeds to VAXes
running VMS actually delivered twice the CPU cycle-equivalents
under a full load.  The "secret" was that the mainframes could do
an equivalent amount of I/O for fewer CPU cycles, and, they
could do more of it.  And, the VAX was not half-bad at I/O
compared to some machines, at least if you had the Emulex SBI 
Massbus adaptor...  The VAX bigots countered, of course, that
since VAX CPU's were cheaper, you could afford to waste a few
cycles when you did I/O.  Were they cheaper?  I doubt it, but
today there is no question that uP cycles are cheap, and the
cheaper the box you attach them to, the cheaper they are.

Anyway, today, "mainframe" has come to mean, to many people,
"High software cost, high maintenance, proprietary", and, therefore,
bad.  In short, everything that was bad about the IBM 370/168  ;-)
So, now, instead of "mainframe" we have "server" and 
"superserver".  It is kind of amusing to see the seriousness with
which marketing types and their clients take these terms.  For 
example, Cray Superservers apparently doesn't want to call its new
machine a "mainframe", even though it is apparently capable of 
mainframe-like I/O (if you believe the public information) so
instead it is a "superserver".  Ho hum.  Marketing speak.
(But, I sure wouldn't mind seeing more machines out there with 
its purported I/O capability.)

Kind of like Scientific Management/MBO/Theory Z/TQM ..., or,
Flowcharing/HIPO/Structured Programming/CMM/Object-Oriented 
Programming ...  ;-)

|> 
|> Now it appears that 'mainframe' has come to mean 'a machine with very
|> high I/O cpaacity'.  If so, this entire discussion is moot, isn't it?
|> By definition, a mainframe will do better I/O than a non-mainframe.

True.  But some people question the wisdom of building big-I/O machines,
while others question the wisdom of building workstation farms...  I
think the jury is still out on what the optimal *interconnect* strategies
are for various kinds of computing strategies.

|> 
|> What's a good definition of 'mainframe'?

Is "mainframe" good or bad?  ;-)


My definition for today (tomorrow might change) is that a mainframe
is a system which can manage at least 1 Terabyte of online and nearline
storage ("nearline" == directories/inodes online, large &/or old files may 
be archived) and provide at least 97% availability of access to that data
averaged over a year  (that is, maintenance time counts as downtime.)
Sound easy?  How long does it take to fsck all your filesystems after a
disk failure?  etc. etc.

